        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nDistinguish a function value from a limit, justify cancellation on a punctured domain, and compare both directions of approach.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview real-number inequalities, factoring a difference of squares, fractions, function notation and reading coordinates. Be able to distinguish the domain from the set of possible outputs before attempting a limit.\n\n## Intuition first\nA reading can be missing at one instant while nearby readings still tell a coherent story. Separate the question “where are the outputs heading?” from “what value was recorded?”\n\n## Visual exploration\nUse the inline limit graph: move x towards 13 from each side, watch the marker approach height 26, and explain why the unfilled circle remains. The widget plots precisely this simplified rule with its original hole.\n\n## Why this works\nA limit tests outputs at nearby admissible inputs, not the value assigned at the target itself. Factoring creates a product; cancelling a common nonzero factor produces an equivalent rule only where that factor is nonzero. After this domain condition is recorded, the simpler nearby rule can be continuous at the target even when the original quotient is not defined there. A two-sided claim still needs left and right approaches to agree.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Approaching a value\nFor $r(t)=(t^2-169)/(t-13)$ with $t\\ne13$, determine $\\lim_{t\\to13}r(t)$ by algebra. Explain why your result does or does not define $r(13)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $r(t)=(t^2-169)/(t-13)$, state $t\\ne13$ before factoring. On that punctured domain,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t)&=\\frac{(t-13)(t+13)}{t-13}=t+13,\\\\\n\\lim_{t\\to13}r(t)&=26.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nA two-sided finite limit requires agreement from both directions. Sum and product laws require the component limits to exist; a quotient law also needs a nonzero limiting denominator. Algebraic cancellation can replace a rule near the excluded input without defining its missing value.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nThe limiting trend and the recorded point value answer different questions. Keep the original domain visible after simplifying, and describe approach from both sides rather than treating the target as an ordinary input.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not substitute into an undefined quotient and call 0/0 an answer. Do not cancel terms across a sum. A hole is not evidence that a limit fails.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nCan an undefined function value coexist with a finite limit?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes; this example has no value at 13 but limit 26.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $\\lim_{t\\to13}(t^2-169)/(t-13)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nfactor, cancel only for $t\\ne13$, then approach 13 in $t+13$, obtaining 26.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer writes $r(13)=26$ after cancelling. Repair it.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nreplace the equality at 13 by $\\lim_{t\\to13}r(t)=26$; the original domain still excludes 13.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf the recorded point is changed to −8, what remains invariant?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nboth one-sided approaches and their common limit remain 26.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nUse a missing sensor sample to distinguish interpolation from an actual observation. Ask learners to draw a hole and a separately placed point, then explain each symbol.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nBefore moving the slider, predict whether approaching from the left gives outputs smaller or larger than 26.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nsmaller, because t+13 is increasing.\n:::end"
