        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nSeparate input infinity from infinite output and support asymptote claims with dominant powers and one-sided signs.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview real-number inequalities, factoring a difference of squares, fractions, function notation and reading coordinates. Be able to distinguish the domain from the set of possible outputs before attempting a limit.\n\n## Intuition first\nAn input growing without bound and an output growing without bound are different stories. Name which quantity is approaching a boundary before applying a familiar rule.\n\n## Visual exploration\nConstruct a sign-and-height sketch of the two reciprocal functions on either side of x=6. Draw a dashed vertical line, then place a negative left branch for p and positive branches for the squared denominator. This manual visual is not the inline quadratic widget.\n\n## Why this works\nFor a rational function at large inputs, dividing by the largest relevant power separates leading behaviour from terms that vanish. Near a finite denominator zero, magnitude alone is insufficient: the numerator and denominator signs determine the direction of unbounded output. A horizontal asymptote concerns end behaviour, while a vertical asymptote concerns an input boundary. Neither is a licence to treat infinity as a real number in an ordinary substitution.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Infinity and asymptotes\nFind the end limit of $q(x)=(4x^2+7)/(2x^2+9)$ as $x\\to\\infty$. For $p(x)=1/(x-6)$, determine the two one-sided behaviours at 6. Compare with $1/(x-6)^2$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $q(x)=(4x^2+7)/(2x^2+9)$, divide by $x^2$ to obtain\n$$\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}q(x)=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{4+7/x^2}{2+9/x^2}=2.$$\nFor $p(x)=1/(x-6)$, a sign table gives $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to6^-$ and $p(x)\\to+\\infty$ as $x\\to6^+$. For $1/(x-6)^2$, both punctured sides are positive and both approach $+\\infty$. An infinite limit describes unbounded behaviour, not a finite real number.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nAn asymptote describes an approach, not necessarily a barrier. One-sided sign evidence must support any two-sided infinite-limit statement.\n## Common mistakes\nInfinity is not a real input to substitute algebraically. Opposite infinite one-sided behaviours cannot be combined into one two-sided positive infinite limit. Keep the sign of a small denominator visible.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nIs a vertical asymptote enough to assert a two-sided positive infinite limit?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; one-sided signs must be checked.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}(4x^2+7)/(2x^2+9)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ndivide by $x^2$ again; the limit is 2.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer says the reciprocal denominator is always positive near 6.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nx−6 is negative on the left; only its square is positive on both punctured sides.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFor $s(x)=-2/(x-6)^2$, describe both approaches.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe positive small denominator and negative numerator give $-\\infty$ from both sides.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nNear-singular models can warn that a formula is leaving its valid operating range. Treat unbounded output as a mathematical prediction to evaluate, not automatically a physical event.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nFor q, does changing x from a large positive to a large negative value change the end limit?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno, because both leading powers are even.\n:::end"
