        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nState domains of nested formulas and verify inverse relationships through their domains and compositions.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview real-number inequalities, factoring a difference of squares, fractions, function notation and reading coordinates. Be able to distinguish the domain from the set of possible outputs before attempting a limit.\n\n## Intuition first\nA formula admits some inputs and refuses others. An inverse asks an additional question: can one recover exactly one input from each admissible output?\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw y=4x−9 and y=(x+9)/4 and reflect points across y=x. For the square function, erase the negative-input half before reflecting; identify the restricted domain and range on the axes.\n\n## Why this works\nEvery operation adds admissibility conditions: a real square root needs a nonnegative input, while a real logarithm needs a strictly positive input. A composition must satisfy the inner operation and the outer operation at the same time. To reverse a rule, solve for the original input and check that each output identifies a unique admissible input. Composition identities verify an inverse on its stated domain; a reciprocal is a different operation.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Domains and reversible descriptions\nDetermine the real domains of $p(x)=\\sqrt{17-2x}$ and $h(x)=\\ln\\sqrt{17-2x}$. Find and verify the inverse of $c(x)=4x-9$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $p(x)=\\sqrt{17-2x}$, real outputs require $x\\le17/2$. For $h(x)=\\ln\\sqrt{17-2x}$, the logarithm needs strict positivity, so $x<17/2$.\nIf $c(x)=4x-9$, undo subtraction before multiplication: $c^{-1}(y)=(y+9)/4$. Checking both compositions gives $c(c^{-1}(y))=y$ and $c^{-1}(c(x))=x$.\n:::end\n\n### Standard reference facts\nFor $f(x)=x^2$, an inverse function exists on the restricted domain $x\\ge0$, with inverse $\\sqrt y$ for $y\\ge0$. It is not the reciprocal $1/x^2$.\n\n## Interpretation\nDomain conditions belong to the function rather than to one chosen algebraic form. A proposed inverse needs both an algebraic check and a one-to-one/domain check.\n## Common mistakes\nA logarithm cannot accept zero. Inverse notation does not mean reciprocal. Solving an algebraic equation without checking the original domain can introduce inadmissible candidates.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy does the logarithmic composition exclude x=17/2?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nits square-root output would be zero and the logarithm is undefined there.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $c^{-1}(19)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$(19+9)/4=7$, and $c(7)=19$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner writes $f^{-1}(y)=1/y^2$ for the restricted square function.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nsolving $y=x^2$ with $x\\ge0$ gives $x=\\sqrt y$, not a reciprocal.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nDetermine the domain of $\\ln(23-4x)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$23-4x>0$, hence $x<23/4$.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nUse unit conversions to explain reversing a calibration. A teaching suggestion is to test proposed inverses by sending one value through both operations, then discuss why one trial is a check rather than a general proof.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan a domain restriction make a noninvertible rule invertible?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes, if the restriction makes the rule one-to-one.\n:::end"
