        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nChoose one-sided branch reasoning or rationalisation according to the obstruction in a limit problem.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview real-number inequalities, factoring a difference of squares, fractions, function notation and reading coordinates. Be able to distinguish the domain from the set of possible outputs before attempting a limit.\n\n## Intuition first\nA road can approach a junction from two directions while following different rules. Work on each side before claiming that the meeting point has one shared trend.\n\n## Visual exploration\nSketch the two horizontal branches of F with open endpoints at x=8. Separately plot a few values of the rationalised function near zero and identify the common height 0.3. Label these as two different examples.\n\n## Why this works\nAn absolute value changes its algebraic description when its argument changes sign. Solve each side on its own domain before comparing the resulting limits. A radical difference can instead be multiplied by its conjugate: the difference-of-squares identity removes the subtraction without changing the quotient where the multiplying denominator is nonzero. The transformed expression can then support an ordinary limit law. These methods address different mathematical obstacles.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — One-sided reasoning and rationalisation\nAnalyse the two-sided limit of $F(x)=|x-8|/(x-8)$ at 8. Separately evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to0}(\\sqrt{25+3x}-5)/x$ by rationalisation. Explain why the methods differ.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $F(x)=|x-8|/(x-8)$ with $x\\ne8$, the left branch equals −1 and the right branch equals 1, so the two-sided limit at 8 does not exist.\nA different indeterminate quotient benefits from a conjugate:\n$$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sqrt{25+3x}-5}{x}=\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25+3x}+5}=\\frac3{10}.$$\nThe cancellation uses $x\\ne0$, and the real domain contains a neighbourhood of zero. The denominator after rationalising approaches 10, so the quotient law now applies.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA removable algebraic obstacle can conceal an existing limit. A genuine disagreement between branch approaches cannot be repaired by assigning one point value.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not replace absolute value by its positive-side rule on the negative side. When rationalising, multiply numerator and denominator by the entire conjugate. Keep the factor 3 after cancellation.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich example has an existing two-sided limit?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe radical quotient does; F has side limits −1 and 1.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nEvaluate the radical limit.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nmultiplying by the conjugate gives $3/(\\sqrt{25+3x}+5)$ for $x\\ne0$, hence $3/10$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer cancels x and reports $1/10$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe numerator difference of squares is $3x$, so the remaining numerator is 3.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFor $|x-8|/(8-x)$, find both side limits at 8.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe left limit is 1 and the right is −1; the two-sided limit still fails.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nA classroom error-sort activity can contrast “method needed” with “limit does not exist.” This is an original teaching suggestion, not an official assessment policy.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nWould assigning F(8)=0 make the two-sided limit exist?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno, because the directional disagreement remains.\n:::end"
