        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nUse continuity to establish a root bracket and a separate monotonicity argument to establish uniqueness.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview real-number inequalities, factoring a difference of squares, fractions, function notation and reading coordinates. Be able to distinguish the domain from the set of possible outputs before attempting a limit.\n\n## Intuition first\nIf a continuous path starts below a height and ends above it, it must cross that height somewhere. This promises a crossing, not its exact location or uniqueness.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw an unbroken rising curve through the endpoint heights −8 and 1. Shade the vertical interval between them and mark the horizontal axis crossing. Contrast this with two separate branches of 1/x.\n\n## Why this works\nThe intermediate value theorem applies to a continuous function on an entire closed interval. Every height between the endpoint heights must occur somewhere inside when it differs from both endpoint values. This establishes existence without locating an exact root. Uniqueness needs more evidence, such as strict increase proved from a positive derivative. Bisection preserves a sign-changing bracket and narrows uncertainty; its midpoint arithmetic is not itself a proof of uniqueness.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Continuity as an existence argument\nFor $P(x)=x^3+2x-11$, justify a root between 1 and 2, decide whether it is unique, and perform one bisection step. State the theorem hypotheses used.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nLet $P(x)=x^3+2x-11$. It is continuous on $[1,2]$, and $P(1)=-8$, $P(2)=1$. The intermediate value theorem therefore guarantees a root in $(1,2)$. Since $P'(x)=3x^2+2>0$ for every real x, strict increase adds uniqueness.\nA first bisection step gives $P(3/2)=27/8+3-11=-37/8<0$, so the root lies in $(3/2,2)$. Sign changes locate narrower brackets; they do not create an exact solution.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nKeep existence, uniqueness and numerical location separate. A graph can suggest all three, but each conclusion needs the appropriate argument.\n## Common mistakes\nA sign change across a discontinuity is insufficient. For example, 1/x changes sign across zero but has no zero. A decimal estimate is not an exact algebraic root.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich hypothesis fails for 1/x on [−1,1]?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ncontinuity on the entire closed interval fails at zero.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nAfter the first bisection step, give the new root bracket.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$P(3/2)<0<P(2)$, so $(3/2,2)$ remains a valid bracket.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA student claims IVT proves exactly one root.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIVT alone proves at least one; here the positive derivative supplies the separate uniqueness argument.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf a continuous f satisfies $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=10$, must it attain 7?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes, for some input between a and b; uniqueness is not guaranteed.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nExistence arguments are useful before attempting numerical solvers. A teaching suggestion is to distinguish a theorem-backed bracket from a graph-screen guess.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCould a continuous function with equal positive endpoint values still have a root inside?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes; same-sign endpoints do not rule out interior crossings.\n:::end"
