        "title": "Use a tangent as a local model",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "linearisation",
          "differentials",
          "approximation"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nBuild and verify a tangent approximation and use concavity to explain the direction of its error.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview the limit ideas from the previous unit, expansion of a binomial square, polynomial arithmetic and the meaning of a rate. Be ready to state excluded inputs rather than cancelling them silently.\n\n## Intuition first\nA tangent is a small-neighbourhood model. It can be useful without being exact, just as a short straight segment can approximate a gently bending route.\n\n## Visual exploration\nPlot the points (64,4) and (65,193/48) on the tangent. Sketch a concave-down cube-root curve just beneath the second point. Zooming out should visibly weaken the straight-line approximation.\n\n## Why this works\nA differentiable function has a first-order local model built from its value and slope at one reference input. This model predicts nearby output changes; it does not claim global equality with the original curve. Concavity compares the curve with its tangent on a suitable interval and can determine which lies above the other. That sign information is weaker than an exact numerical error bound, which needs further justification.\n\n## Procedure\nUse $L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)$. \n\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Use a tangent as a local model\nUse a tangent model for $f(x)=x^{1/3}$ near 64 to estimate $\\sqrt[3]{65}$. Explain why the chosen reference point is convenient and justify whether the estimate is above or below the exact value.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $f(x)=x^{1/3}$ near a=64, $f(64)=4$ and $f'(64)=1/48$. Therefore\n$$L(x)=4+\\frac{x-64}{48},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{65}\\approx L(65)=\\frac{193}{48}.$$\nOn positive inputs, $f''(x)=-2/(9x^{5/3})<0$, so the tangent lies above this concave-down curve. The differential relation $dy=f'(a)\\,dx$ predicts a change; it is not generally the exact finite change.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nAn approximation should carry an approximation sign and a stated neighbourhood of intended use. A geometric direction-of-error argument does not establish a precise error magnitude.\n## Common mistakes\nUse an approximation sign. A tangent that works near 64 is not thereby reliable near 1000. Do not confuse the predicted change dy with the new output f(a)+dy.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy choose a=64?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nits cube root and derivative value are exact and easy to compute.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nEstimate $\\sqrt[3]{63}$ with the same model.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nInsert x=63 into the same tangent model: $L(63)=4+(63-64)/48=4-1/48=191/48$. This remains an approximation, not an exact cube root.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner says the 65 estimate is exact.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe straight tangent agrees to first order at 64; use ≈, and verify by cubing that 193/48 is not an exact cube root of 65.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nLinearise $\\sqrt x$ at 81 and estimate $\\sqrt{82}$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$L(x)=9+(x-81)/18$, giving $163/18$.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nLocal models support quick engineering estimates when their range is stated. A teaching suggestion is to compare one nearby and one distant input to make “local” operational.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf the cube-root curve is concave down, should the tangent estimate lie above it?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes, on this positive-input interval.\n:::end"
