        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nExplain the inserted-zero argument in the product-rule proof and distinguish its hypotheses from its conclusion.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview the limit ideas from the previous unit, expansion of a binomial square, polynomial arithmetic and the meaning of a rate. Be ready to state excluded inputs rather than cancelling them silently.\n\n## Intuition first\nA remembered rule is easier to trust when you can rebuild its main idea. Introduce one extra term so a changing product can be separated into two familiar rates.\n\n## Visual exploration\nWrite the product difference in two colours: first hold u(a+h) fixed while v changes, then hold v(a) fixed while u changes. Draw arrows from these two changes to the corresponding derivative contributions.\n\n## Why this works\nAdding and subtracting the same intermediate product changes the organisation of a difference without changing its value. The reorganised terms isolate one difference quotient for each changing factor. Differentiability supplies those limits and also supplies the continuity needed for an undifferentiated factor. Each limiting step therefore has a hypothesis, rather than being a purely formal replacement of nearby inputs by the target input.\n\n## Procedure\n### Standard reference derivation\nFor differentiable u and v at a, add and subtract $u(a+h)v(a)$ in the product difference:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{u(a+h)v(a+h)-u(a)v(a)}h\n&=u(a+h)\\frac{v(a+h)-v(a)}h\\\\\n&\\quad+v(a)\\frac{u(a+h)-u(a)}h.\n\\end{aligned}$$\nDifferentiability implies continuity, so taking h→0 gives $u(a)v'(a)+v(a)u'(a)$. As a smaller proof, $[(a+h)^2-a^2]/h=2a+h$ for h≠0, yielding $(x^2)'=2x$.\n\n## Interpretation\nA diagram can motivate the product rule, while the limit argument explains the neglected small contribution. A valid proof makes the use of continuity and derivative limits explicit.\n## Common mistakes\nAdding a term requires subtracting the identical term as well. Do not assume u(a+h)=u(a) before taking the limit. A limit of a product requires the relevant limits to exist.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat justifies u(a+h)→u(a)?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nu is differentiable at a and therefore continuous there.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nUse first principles for $5x^2+9$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe quotient becomes $10a+5h$ at a, so the derivative is $10a$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA proof adds u(a+h)v(a) without subtracting it.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthis changes the expression; insert a zero as one added and one subtracted identical quantity.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf u has units metres and v has units metres, what units does (uv)′ have with time as input?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nsquare metres per second; both product-rule contributions have those units.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nIn teacher training, ask a learner to annotate a proof with the justification for each arrow rather than merely reciting a formula. This is an original PCK suggestion.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIs differentiability of u and v stronger than the continuity needed in one proof step?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes, and that stronger hypothesis supplies both continuity and their derivative limits.\n:::end"
