        "title": "A differentiation diagnostic workshop",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "rule selection",
          "domain checks",
          "error correction"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nUse division and a sign check to verify a rational derivative while retaining excluded inputs.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview the limit ideas from the previous unit, expansion of a binomial square, polynomial arithmetic and the meaning of a rate. Be ready to state excluded inputs rather than cancelling them silently.\n\n## Intuition first\nChoosing the first valid move is often harder than carrying out the algebra. Inspect the outer operation and the domain before reaching for a familiar derivative formula.\n\n## Visual exploration\nCreate a sign chart for J′: it is zero at 3±√20 and undefined at 3. Mark the intervals where its numerator and denominator imply positive or negative slope before drawing a qualitative curve.\n\n## Why this works\nAlgebraically equivalent formulas on the same domain must have the same derivative wherever they are differentiable. Polynomial division can expose a linear term plus a reciprocal term, making a second derivation possible. A derivative sign chart then checks whether local slopes agree with the expected curve direction. Numerical difference quotients are useful diagnostics near an admissible input, but checking finitely many inputs cannot prove a general identity.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — A differentiation diagnostic workshop\nFor $J(x)=(x^2+11)/(x-3)$, derive J′ by polynomial division and verify it against the quotient rule. Evaluate the slope at x=4 and identify all excluded inputs.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $J(x)=(x^2+11)/(x-3)$, division gives $J(x)=x+3+20/(x-3)$ with x≠3. Then\n$$J'(x)=1-\\frac{20}{(x-3)^2}=\\frac{x^2-6x-11}{(x-3)^2}.$$\nThis agrees with the quotient-rule result by a genuinely different route. At x=4, the derivative is −19. A small symmetric difference quotient near 4 should be close to −19, provided the sample inputs remain in the domain.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nIndependent routes help locate sign errors rather than merely repeat them. Neither a symbolic rewrite nor a successful numerical check restores an input excluded from the original function.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not differentiate 1/(x−3) as a positive reciprocal square. Do not cancel the excluded domain point merely because a transformed formula looks simpler.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy is polynomial division useful here?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit separates a linear term from one reciprocal term, giving an independent derivative check.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $J'(4)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$1-20/(4-3)^2=-19$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer gets $1+20/(x-3)^2$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe derivative of $(x-3)^{-1}$ includes the negative exponent, giving a minus sign.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFor $Q(x)=(x^2+18)/(x-4)$, divide then differentiate.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$Q=x+4+34/(x-4)$, so $Q'=1-34/(x-4)^2$, x≠4.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nA useful formative check is to ask for a second method and an excluded input, not just a final derivative. A teaching suggestion is to compare the two students’ unsimplified reasoning before comparing answers.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan a correct numerical slope at one point prove a derivative formula?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; it is a check, not a universal proof.\n:::end"
