        "title": "A connected derivative reference",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "inverse functions",
          "hyperbolic derivatives",
          "domain conditions"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nUse inverse and reciprocal relationships to reconstruct derivative formulas and state their real domains.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview first derivatives, product and quotient rules, function composition and real logarithm domains. Use radians for trigonometric formulas and distinguish a dependent variable from an independent input.\n\n## Intuition first\nA derivative table is easier to rebuild when its entries are connected. Inverse relationships, reciprocals and exponential definitions reduce the number of disconnected rules to remember.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw sin restricted to [−π/2,π/2], then reflect it across y=x to obtain arcsin. Near the reflected endpoints the slope becomes steep; connect that picture to the denominator √(1−x²).\n\n## Why this works\nAn inverse function reverses an input-output relation, while a reciprocal takes the multiplicative inverse of an output. These are different constructions and use different derivative arguments. Hyperbolic functions can be rebuilt from their exponential definitions, then extended through quotient and reciprocal rules. Domain restrictions must stay attached to the formulas: a function can be defined at an endpoint where its ordinary derivative is not finite.\n\n## Procedure\nFor x≠0, $(\\ln|x|)'=1/x$. Inverse rules give $(\\arcsin x)'=1/\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ and $(\\arccos x)'=-1/\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ for |x|<1, while $(\\arctan x)'=1/(1+x^2)$ for all real x.\nFrom $\\sinh x=(e^x-e^{-x})/2$ and $\\cosh x=(e^x+e^{-x})/2$, their derivatives are cosh x and sinh x. \n\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — A connected derivative reference\nLet $w(x)=\\operatorname{sech}(5x+11)$. Differentiate w by combining the reciprocal hyperbolic rule with the chain rule. Identify the source of each factor and sign.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nReciprocals give\n$$[\\operatorname{sech}(5x+11)]'=-5\\operatorname{sech}(5x+11)\\tanh(5x+11).$$\n:::end\n\n### Standard reference facts\nThe derivative of $a^x$ is $a^x\\ln a$ for a>0. The case a=1 is the constant function and gives derivative zero.\n\n## Interpretation\nReference identities are standard mathematics, not newly invented rules. Successful recall includes knowing where a rule applies and distinguishing inverse notation from reciprocal notation.\n## Common mistakes\nInverse and reciprocal functions are different. The reciprocal hyperbolic secant is not an inverse hyperbolic function. Do not replace the inverse-cosine derivative’s negative sign by a positive one.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhere is the displayed arcsin derivative finite?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe derivative denominator is positive and finite only when −1<x<1. At either endpoint it vanishes, so the displayed finite derivative formula does not apply.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $[\\arcsin(x/5)]'$ for |x|<5.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$(1/5)/\\sqrt{1-x^2/25}=1/\\sqrt{25-x^2}$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner omits the 5 in the sech example.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nits outer derivative must be multiplied by the inner rate 5.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFind $[\\ln|7x-2|]'$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$7/(7x-2)$ for x≠2/7.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nCreate a reference network where every arrow names a rule and every formula lists a domain. A PCK suggestion is to ask which apparent “memorisation error” is actually a domain misconception.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan the arcsin derivative be evaluated at x=1 as a finite number?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno, even though arcsin itself is defined there.\n:::end"
