        "title": "Tangent constraints and inverse reasoning",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "inverse derivative theorem",
          "implicit slopes",
          "local branches"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nEvaluate an inverse derivative at the correct output and connect implicit slopes to local branch conditions.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview first derivatives, product and quotient rules, function composition and real logarithm domains. Use radians for trigonometric formulas and distinguish a dependent variable from an independent input.\n\n## Intuition first\nReversing a smooth calibration interchanges input and output. Its local rate should reverse as well, provided the original rate is nonzero.\n\n## Visual exploration\nPlot the small tangent rise/run at (2,16), then exchange the axes to see run/rise at (16,2). Label the corresponding slopes 16 and 1/16. Separately sketch the ellipse tangent at (6,3).\n\n## Why this works\nA differentiable inverse interchanges input and output, so a nonzero local rate is reciprocated at the corresponding exchanged point. The inverse derivative is evaluated at the original output, while the original derivative must be evaluated at its preimage. This argument needs a valid local inverse and a nonzero original derivative. Implicit slope formulas similarly describe local behaviour only where their solving step is valid; a global curve need not be one graph branch.\n\n## Procedure\nIf a differentiable one-to-one function f has f(a)=b and f′(a)≠0 with the relevant local inverse differentiable, then $(f^{-1})'(b)=1/f'(a)$.\n\n\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Tangent constraints and inverse reasoning\nFor $f(x)=x^3+4x$, determine $(f^{-1})^{\\prime}(16)$ using the matching preimage. Also find the tangent to $x^2+2y^2=54$ at (6,3), after checking that the point lies on the curve.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $f(x)=x^3+4x$, f(2)=16 and f′(2)=16, hence $(f^{-1})'(16)=1/16$.\nFor $x^2+2y^2=54$, differentiation yields $y'=-x/(2y)$ when y≠0. At (6,3), the point equation holds and the slope is −1, so the tangent is $y-3=-(x-6)$.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nAlways label which coordinate belongs to the original input and which belongs to its output. Reciprocal rates require their own hypotheses; they do not follow from notation alone.\n## Common mistakes\nA reciprocal rate is not the derivative of a reciprocal function. Do not substitute b into f′ when the theorem needs the preimage a. An implicit formula may describe several branches globally.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich point is used on the inverse graph?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n(16,2), with the coordinates exchanged.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $(f^{-1})'(0)$ for the same f.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nf(0)=0 and f′(0)=4, so the result is 1/4.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA student reports 1/f′(16).\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe inverse derivative at output 16 uses its preimage 2, so compute 1/f′(2).\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFor $g(x)=2x^3+5x$, find $(g^{-1})'(7)$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ng(1)=7 and g′(1)=11, giving 1/11.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nInverse calibration sensitivity matters when converting measured outputs back into estimated inputs. A teaching suggestion is to make the exchanged coordinates explicit before applying a formula.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf f′(a)=0, may this reciprocal formula simply return infinity?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; its nonzero-derivative hypothesis fails and a separate inverse analysis is needed.\n:::end"
