        "title": "A multi-step derivative investigation",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "logarithmic differentiation",
          "chain rule",
          "verification"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nPlan logarithmic differentiation and justify any extension beyond the method’s temporary domain.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview first derivatives, product and quotient rules, function composition and real logarithm domains. Use radians for trigonometric formulas and distinguish a dependent variable from an independent input.\n\n## Intuition first\nAn expression with several changing factors rewards a plan. Decide which positive factors permit logarithms, then use the logarithm to separate products into manageable rates.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw a dependency tree with branches x³ and √(x²+6), then write a product-rule contribution along each branch. Compare the two resulting terms with the logarithmic factors 3/x and x/(x²+6).\n\n## Why this works\nLogarithms separate products into sums and powers into multipliers, but a real logarithm cannot be used indiscriminately. Record the domain on which every logarithmic step is legitimate. After simplifying, a resulting derivative expression may make sense on a wider domain; that observation alone does not prove the derivative there. A direct product-and-chain derivation, or a separate definition-based argument at excluded points, supplies the missing justification.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — A multi-step derivative investigation\nFor $Y(x)=x^3\\sqrt{x^2+6}$, derive Y′ using logarithms on x>0. Then use a direct product-and-chain argument to determine whether the simplified derivative is valid on the original function’s full real domain.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $Y(x)=x^3\\sqrt{x^2+6}$ on x>0, write\n$$\\ln Y=3\\ln x+\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+6).$$\nDifferentiating and multiplying by Y gives\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nY'&=x^3\\sqrt{x^2+6}\\left(\\frac3x+\\frac{x}{x^2+6}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{x^2(4x^2+18)}{\\sqrt{x^2+6}}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\nThe original function is actually real on all real x. A direct product-and-chain derivation verifies the final derivative on that full domain, including x=0. The logarithmic route alone was justified only for x>0.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA method’s domain can be narrower than the domain of the original problem. Extending a conclusion requires a fresh validity argument rather than erasing the temporary restriction.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not take ln Y where Y is negative or zero without changing the argument. The derivative of ln(x²+6) includes 2x. A correct-looking simplified expression is not evidence that every intermediate step was valid everywhere.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy did the logarithmic route use x>0?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit made both x and Y positive, so their real logarithms were legitimate.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind Y′(1).\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$(4+18)/\\sqrt7=22/\\sqrt7$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner claims the logarithmic derivation proves the result at x=0.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthose logarithms were undefined there; verify x=0 by a direct derivative route.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nDifferentiate $Z(x)=x^2\\sqrt{x^2+10}$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nproduct and chain rules give $Z'=x(3x^2+20)/\\sqrt{x^2+10}$ for all real x.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nA formative PCK task can ask learners to annotate where a method is valid, not merely produce a derivative. This distinguishes a procedural success from a genuine domain understanding.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf two derivations agree on x>0, does that alone prove their extensions to x<0?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; establish validity on that additional domain.\n:::end"
