        "title": "Optimisation with an admissible domain",
        "visual": "optimization",
        "concepts": [
          "optimisation",
          "constraints",
          "global justification"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nTranslate a resource constraint into an admissible objective and justify a global optimum with units.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview derivative rules, inequalities, sign charts and endpoint evaluation. Be able to connect the sign of a derivative to a local rate before using it to make a global claim.\n\n## Intuition first\nA best mathematical choice is useful only when it is feasible. Turn the resource constraint into a domain before solving a stationary equation.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw the wall as one long edge, then label the two perpendicular sides x and the opposite side 84−2x. Plot the downward parabola A on 0<x<42, marking its vertex (21,882).\n\n## Why this works\nOptimisation begins with a feasible set, not with differentiation. A resource equation can eliminate one variable and express the objective using a single admissible input. A stationary candidate must then be tested against the objective’s shape, derivative signs or endpoint behaviour to justify a global conclusion. Finally translate the selected input back into every requested dimension and its units. An optimum of the wrong constraint answers a different problem.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Optimisation with an admissible domain\nA rectangle against a straight wall uses 84 m of edging on its other three sides. Let each side perpendicular to the wall have length x. Determine the feasible domain and the dimensions giving the largest area, and justify global optimality.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nA rectangular teaching garden uses 84 m of edging along three sides against a wall. If each perpendicular side is x, the remaining side is 84−2x, so\n$$A(x)=x(84-2x),\\qquad 0<x<42.$$\nThen A′=84−4x vanishes at x=21. The remaining side is 42 m, and $A(21)=882\\text{ m}^2$. Since A″=−4 everywhere and the area tends to zero at each limiting endpoint, this candidate is the global maximum on the feasible interval.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nFeasibility, stationarity and global optimality are distinct checks. A physical design conclusion must include every dimension requested and a justification that its resource constraint is the intended one.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not use 2x+2y=84 when one side needs no edging. Reject lengths that make the other dimension nonpositive. A root of A′ outside the domain would not describe a garden.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy is the domain open?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nzero-length sides are not admissible rectangular gardens.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nIf x=18, find the area.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe third side is 48, so the area is 864 square metres.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner reports x=42 as the optimum because it is the largest input.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit leaves the third side zero and gives no admissible garden.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nRepeat with 100 m of edging.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$A=x(100-2x)$, so x=25, third side=50 and maximum area=1250 square metres.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nOptimisation should end with quantities, units and an explanation of feasibility. A PCK suggestion is to have learners critique a technically correct derivative applied to an incorrect constraint.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nWould the same optimum apply if all four sides needed edging?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; the resource equation and feasible objective would change.\n:::end"
