        "title": "Assemble a complete rational-curve story",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "rational curves",
          "oblique asymptote",
          "domain exclusions"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nAssemble a coherent rational-curve sketch from domain, asymptotes, derivative signs and concavity.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview derivative rules, inequalities, sign charts and endpoint evaluation. Be able to connect the sign of a derivative to a local rate before using it to make a global claim.\n\n## Intuition first\nA curve sketch is a set of claims that must agree: allowed inputs, intercepts, asymptotes, turning points and curvature. Let each independent check constrain the final picture.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw both asymptotes first, mark (0,−9/2), then the two critical points. Add branch directions and concavity arrows before joining the permitted portions of the graph.\n\n## Why this works\nPolynomial division separates a rational curve into a simple long-range trend plus a remainder that controls its deviation from an asymptote. First-derivative signs constrain where each branch rises or falls; second-derivative signs constrain how the branches bend. Domain exclusions prevent otherwise tempting connections between branches. A concavity sign change across a missing input is not an inflection, because an inflection must be a point on the graph.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Assemble a complete rational-curve story\nFor $R(x)=(x^2+9)/(x-2)$, determine the domain, intercepts, vertical and oblique asymptotes, critical points and concavity. Combine these features into a justified sketch.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $R(x)=(x^2+9)/(x-2)$, division gives\n$$R=x+2+\\frac{13}{x-2},\\quad R'=1-\\frac{13}{(x-2)^2},\\quad R''=\\frac{26}{(x-2)^3}.$$\nThe domain excludes 2, R(0)=−9/2, and no real x-intercept exists. The asymptotes are x=2 and y=x+2. Critical inputs are $2\\pm\\sqrt{13}$: the left one is a local maximum with value $4-2\\sqrt{13}$; the right is a local minimum with value $4+2\\sqrt{13}$. Concavity is down on the left branch and up on the right.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nEach curve feature is a constraint on the final drawing. If a proposed sketch contradicts any domain, sign or asymptote calculation, revise it rather than averaging conflicting evidence.\n## Common mistakes\nA sloping asymptote comes from the division remainder tending to zero; it is not a horizontal limit. Do not connect the branches across the vertical asymptote. A sign change at an excluded input does not create an inflection.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy is there no horizontal-axis crossing?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nx²+9 is strictly positive for every real input.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind R(3).\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n(9+9)/(3−2)=18.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner labels x=2 an inflection.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nR is undefined there, so there is no point of the graph at which concavity could change.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFor $S(x)=(x^2+16)/(x-3)$, find the oblique asymptote.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ndivision gives $S=x+3+25/(x-3)$, hence y=x+3.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nGraph interpretation should survive without graphing software. A formative PCK activity is to give conflicting sketch features and ask students to identify which cannot coexist.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan the left branch cross y=0?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno, because the numerator never vanishes.\n:::end"
