        "title": "Mean value reasoning and uniqueness",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "Rolle theorem",
          "mean value theorem",
          "monotonicity"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nApply the mean value theorem with explicit hypotheses and use it to justify bounds or monotonicity.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview derivative rules, inequalities, sign charts and endpoint evaluation. Be able to connect the sign of a derivative to a local rate before using it to make a global claim.\n\n## Intuition first\nAn average slope across a smooth journey must occur as an instantaneous slope somewhere inside it. This connects a global change with local information under explicit smoothness conditions.\n\n## Visual exploration\nSketch the secant joining (1,4) to (4,28), then draw the tangent at x=5/2 with the same slope 8. Label the endpoints and the strictly interior mean value point separately.\n\n## Why this works\nFor a continuous function on a closed interval that is differentiable inside, the mean value theorem connects total endpoint change to a derivative at some interior input. It guarantees existence, not necessarily uniqueness. Equal endpoint heights give Rolle’s theorem as a special case. Applying the theorem to any two inputs also turns a derivative sign condition into a global monotonicity claim, or a derivative bound into a bound on total change.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Mean value reasoning and uniqueness\nFor $f(x)=x^2+3x$ on [1,4], find every mean value point and check that it lies in the required open interval. Explain which hypotheses support the calculation.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $f(x)=x^2+3x$ on [1,4], the average slope is (28−4)/3=8. Since f′=2x+3, the mean value point is c=5/2, which lies in (1,4).\nRolle’s theorem is the equal-endpoint case: continuity on [a,b], differentiability on (a,b), and f(a)=f(b) guarantee an interior c with f′(c)=0.\nIf f′>0 throughout an interval, applying the mean value theorem to any pair of inputs shows that f is strictly increasing there.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA theorem-backed existence claim is stronger than a graph-screen guess. Distinguish finding an input satisfying the slope equation from establishing why such an input must exist.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not apply the theorem across a hole or corner without checking the required smoothness. A picture suggesting a parallel tangent is intuition; the hypotheses justify the conclusion.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat extra endpoint condition yields Rolle’s theorem?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nRolle’s theorem adds equal endpoint values to the continuity and interior differentiability hypotheses. Their secant slope is then zero, so the mean value derivative is zero.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind the mean value point for $g(x)=x^2+5x$ on [2,6].\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\naverage slope is 13, so 2c+5=13 and c=4.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer says every continuous function has a mean value tangent.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ndifferentiability throughout the open interval is also required.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf h′(x)≥4 everywhere on [1,3] with the theorem’s hypotheses, bound h(3)−h(1).\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit is at least 4·2=8.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nUse mean value reasoning to bound accumulated change from a lower rate bound. A teaching suggestion is to separate “locate c” tasks from “prove some c exists” tasks.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCould more than one mean value point exist?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nyes; the general theorem does not promise uniqueness.\n:::end"
