        "title": "Optimisation and limit method selection",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "open-top box",
          "constraint modelling",
          "limit transformations"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nChoose valid modelling and limit methods before executing algebraic calculations.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview derivative rules, inequalities, sign charts and endpoint evaluation. Be able to connect the sign of a derivative to a local rate before using it to make a global claim.\n\n## Intuition first\nBefore calculating, classify the task: maximise a feasible quantity or analyse an approach. A plausible algebraic procedure can answer the wrong question if that classification is skipped.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw the sheet with four corner cuts and label the folded dimensions x,30−2x,18−2x. Separately draw x ln x on positive inputs approaching the open endpoint (0,0); do not combine these unrelated graphs.\n\n## Why this works\nA modelled volume requires all physical dimensions to remain positive, so algebraic roots must be filtered through the original feasible domain. A sign change of the derivative can then justify the selected root as a maximum. Limit methods have their own entry conditions: a product is not directly a quotient indeterminate form, but an equivalent quotient on the same approach domain may allow an applicable theorem. Preserve that domain through the transformation.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Optimisation and limit method selection\nCut squares of side x from the corners of a 30 cm by 18 cm sheet and fold an open box. Find the feasible volume-maximising cut size and justify it. Separately determine $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}x\\ln x$ with an eligible limit method.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nCut squares of side x from a 30 cm by 18 cm sheet and fold an open box. Its volume is\n$$V(x)=x(30-2x)(18-2x),\\qquad 0<x<9.$$\nExpanding and differentiating gives $V'=12x^2-192x+540=12(x^2-16x+45)$. Candidates are $x=8\\pm\\sqrt{19}$; only $8-\\sqrt{19}$ is feasible. V′ changes from positive to negative there and the volume tends to zero at both limiting endpoints, proving a global maximum.\nFor a separate limit, rewrite $x\\ln x=\\ln x/(1/x)$ for x>0. As x→0+, the form is −∞/∞; the derivative quotient is −x, so the limit is zero by L’Hôpital’s hypotheses.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA valid manipulation must also address the original question. Feasible modelling and justified limit transformations are choices to explain, not merely preliminary algebra.\n## Common mistakes\nThe larger root makes a negative box dimension. Do not silently extend x ln x to negative real inputs. The limit of x ln x at zero does not define its original value there.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich stationary root is feasible?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n8−√19, approximately 3.64, lies in (0,9).\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind the box volume at x=3.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n3·24·12=864 cubic centimetres.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner uses x=8+√19 for the box.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit exceeds 9 and makes 18−2x negative, so it violates the physical domain.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to0^+}x^2\\ln x$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nwrite $\\ln x/(1/x^2)$; the derivative quotient is $-x^2/2$, tending to zero.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nAn assessment can ask students to reject a wrong method before computing. This original PCK suggestion tests modelling judgement and theorem selection, not merely symbolic fluency.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIs a larger stationary input automatically a larger feasible objective?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; admissibility and objective values must both be checked.\n:::end"
