        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nCoordinate a substitution’s inner expression, differential and endpoint transformation, then verify the result.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview differentiation, finite sums, function signs, interval notation and the meaning of an initial condition. Be able to distinguish a rate from a total and track the units of both.\n\n## Intuition first\nA nested expression can provide a more natural measuring variable. Substitution succeeds when the differential changes along with the inner quantity.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw a two-row correspondence table: x=0 maps to u=7; x=2 maps to u=19; a small interval dx maps to du=6x dx. Use arrows to connect bounds before integrating.\n\n## Why this works\nSubstitution reverses a chain-rule structure rather than merely renaming a variable. Identify a changing inner quantity and express the available factor as its differential, including any constant multiplier. A definite integral must use endpoints measured in the new variable, or return fully to the original variable before evaluation. Differentiating an indefinite result checks whether the missing inner-rate factor has been correctly compensated.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Substitution and a final transfer check\nEvaluate $\\int_0^2 6x(3x^2+7)^2\\,dx$ by substitution, showing the differential and transformed endpoints. Also find antiderivatives of $e^{4x+3}$ and $\\cos(5x-2)$ and check them by differentiation.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nEvaluate $\\int_0^2 6x(3x^2+7)^2\\,dx$. Set u=3x²+7, so du=6x dx; the bounds become 7 and 19:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^2 6x(3x^2+7)^2\\,dx&=\\int_7^{19}u^2\\,du\\\\\n&=\\frac{19^3-7^3}{3}=2172.\n\\end{aligned}$$\nFor indefinite patterns,\n$$\\int e^{4x+3}\\,dx=\\tfrac14e^{4x+3}+C,\\qquad \\int\\cos(5x-2)\\,dx=\\tfrac15\\sin(5x-2)+C.$$\nDifferentiate proposed answers to check the inner-rate factors.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nAll parts of a transformed integral must refer to the same variable. A valid answer is supported by endpoint consistency and a derivative check, not simply by a familiar-looking power.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not retain x bounds on a u integral. The exponential and trigonometric answers need reciprocal inner-rate factors. An indefinite answer should be returned to the original variable unless the context explicitly requests otherwise.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat part of the integrand is the differential of the inner function?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor $u=3x^2+7$, differentiate to obtain $du/dx=6x$, hence $du=6x\\,dx$. This matches the entire outside factor together with the differential.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nIf the outer factor were 3x instead of 6x, evaluate the same definite integral.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nit becomes half of 2172, namely 1086.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner evaluates u³/3 at 0 and 2.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthose are x endpoints; the u endpoints are 7 and 19.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFind $\\int\\cos(7x+4)\\,dx$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$(1/7)\\sin(7x+4)+C$, verified by differentiation.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nUse the reverse chain rule as the explanatory model, rather than “replace a letter.” A teaching suggestion is to require the differential and new-bound table before any antiderivative is taken.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nWhat if du matches only a constant multiple of the available factor?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ncarry that constant explicitly outside the integral.\n:::end"
