        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nDistinguish final position, displacement and distance using initial conditions and sign-aware integration.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview differentiation, finite sums, function signs, interval notation and the meaning of an initial condition. Be able to distinguish a rate from a total and track the units of both.\n\n## Intuition first\nA moving object can return near its starting point after travelling a long route. Direction matters for displacement, while total distance counts all movement positively.\n\n## Visual exploration\nUse the signed rectangles for the velocity model $v(t)=2t-7$ on $[0,6]$. Predict which side of the zero crossing at $t=7/2$ contributes more distance. Then compare the signed estimate with the magnitudes of the two regions.\n\n:::widget{type=\"riemann_sum\" quadratic=0 linear=2 constant=-7 start=0 end=6}\n:::\n\n## Why this works\nIntegrating velocity reconstructs position only up to an additive constant; an initial position determines that constant. A definite integral of velocity adds signed motion and gives displacement. Total distance instead integrates speed, which is the magnitude of velocity. Find any sign-change inputs first, then split the interval so each contribution’s orientation is understood. Changing the initial position shifts the position graph but does not change the velocity graph or its signed areas.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Net change is not total travel\nA robot has $v(t)=2t-7$ m/s on [0,6] and s(0)=11 m. Determine its final position, displacement and total distance, showing how you handle any direction reversal.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nAn inspection robot has velocity v(t)=2t−7 m/s on [0,6], with s(0)=11 m. Integrating and using the initial condition gives\n$$s(t)=t^2-7t+11,\\qquad s(6)=5.$$\nIts displacement is −6 m. Velocity changes sign at t=7/2, so\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^6|2t-7|\\,dt&=-\\int_0^{7/2}(2t-7)\\,dt+\\int_{7/2}^6(2t-7)\\,dt\\\\\n&=\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{25}{4}=\\frac{37}{2}\\text{ m}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\nTaking the absolute value of the final displacement would give only 6 m, which misses the reversal.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nPosition, net change and total movement answer different questions. Choose the accumulation operation from the context before deciding how to handle a negative contribution.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not integrate speed as signed velocity without splitting where the sign changes. The first subinterval contributes positively to distance only after negating its negative signed integral.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich quantity equals 5 m?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe final position, not displacement or distance.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind the position at the reversal.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$s(7/2)=49/4-49/2+11=-5/4$ m.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer reports distance 6 m.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthat is the magnitude of displacement; the correct distance adds both positive travel amounts to give 37/2 m.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf the initial position were 20 m instead, what changes?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nfinal position becomes 14 m; displacement remains −6 m and total distance remains 37/2 m.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nThis distinction transfers to inventory inflow/outflow and charging/discharging as well as motion. A PCK suggestion is to ask for a sketch before allowing an absolute-value shortcut.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes changing the initial position change the areas under velocity?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; velocity and its integrals over the interval are unchanged.\n:::end"
