        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nFind trigonometric, exponential and logarithmic antiderivatives while preserving inner factors, signs and domains.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview differentiation, finite sums, function signs, interval notation and the meaning of an initial condition. Be able to distinguish a rate from a total and track the units of both.\n\n## Intuition first\nAn antiderivative can be tested by sending it through differentiation. Inner-rate factors that were multipliers during differentiation become compensating factors during integration.\n\n## Visual exploration\nSketch sin(3x) on [0,π/6], from height zero to height one. The area is positive, consistent with 1/3. For the exponential example, draw an increasing positive curve so a negative integral result would immediately be suspicious.\n\n## Why this works\nDifferentiating a proposed antiderivative is a direct check of signs and constant factors. The chain rule explains why an inner multiplier in a derivative must be compensated when integrating. For a logarithmic pattern, the numerator must match the denominator’s derivative up to a known factor, and intervals must avoid zeros of the logarithm argument. Positive-integrand and endpoint-orientation checks can also reject a numerical result with an impossible sign.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — Trigonometric and exponential accumulation\nEvaluate $\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\sin(3x)\\,dx$ and $\\int_0^1 5e^{5x+2}\\,dx$. Find an antiderivative of $2x/(x^2+13)$, state its domain, and verify each antiderivative by differentiation.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nOn [0,π/6],\n$$\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\sin(3x)\\,dx=\\left[-\\tfrac13\\cos(3x)\\right]_0^{\\pi/6}=\\tfrac13.$$\nAlso,\n$$\\int_0^1 5e^{5x+2}\\,dx=[e^{5x+2}]_0^1=e^7-e^2.$$\nFor a logarithmic pattern, $\\int 2x/(x^2+13)\\,dx=\\ln(x^2+13)+C$, since the denominator is positive for every real x. In general, $\\int u'(x)/u(x)\\,dx=\\ln|u(x)|+C$ on intervals where u is differentiable and nonzero.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA correct primitive must differentiate back to the entire original integrand. Domain and sign checks supply independent evidence beyond matching a memorised pattern.\n## Common mistakes\nDo not confuse an integral of sin with its derivative. Keep trigonometric angles in radians. A formula involving ln|u| is applied separately on intervals that avoid zeros of u.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy is the sine integral positive?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nthe integrand is nonnegative on the entire interval and positive inside.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $\\int_0^{\\pi/8}\\sin(4x)\\,dx$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$[-\\cos(4x)/4]_0^{\\pi/8}=1/4$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner gives $\\cos(3x)/3$ as the sine antiderivative.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ndifferentiating it gives −sin(3x); the required antiderivative has a minus sign.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nFind $\\int 6x/(3x^2+17)\\,dx$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$\\ln(3x^2+17)+C$, with a positive argument for every real x.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nOscillating and exponential rates can model different kinds of accumulation. A formative PCK check is to pair each proposed antiderivative with its derivative and ask which sign or factor fails.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf the exponential integrand lacked its factor 5, how would the answer change?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\ndivide the antiderivative by 5.\n:::end"
