        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nTranslate enclosed area, average value and integral bounds into the correct accumulation operation.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview differentiation, finite sums, function signs, interval notation and the meaning of an initial condition. Be able to distinguish a rate from a total and track the units of both.\n\n## Intuition first\nA total often becomes meaningful only after comparing it with a baseline or dividing it by the interval length. Decide what each operation measures before computing.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw both curves through (0,0) and (4,16), then shade vertical strips of height 4x−x². Separately draw a horizontal height-nine rectangle over [1,4] whose area equals that under x²+2.\n\n## Why this works\nArea between curves adds their vertical separation over intervals where their ordering is known. Intersections identify candidate boundaries and possible ordering changes; subtract the lower height from the upper on each valid piece. An average value rescales an accumulated total by the interval width and therefore has the same units as the function. Integrating constant lower and upper bounds gives useful limits on a total before an exact calculation is attempted.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Original worked example — An accumulation transfer workshop\nFind the area enclosed by y=4x and y=x². Then find the integral average of f(x)=x²+2 on [1,4], and justify a general bound when m≤f≤M on an interval [a,b] with a<b.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe line y=4x and parabola y=x² meet at x=0 and x=4. On [0,4], the line lies above the parabola, so their enclosed area is\n$$\\int_0^4(4x-x^2)\\,dx=\\left[2x^2-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^4=\\frac{32}{3}.$$\nFor f(x)=x²+2 on [1,4], its average value is\n$$f_{\\mathrm{avg}}=\\frac1{4-1}\\int_1^4(x^2+2)\\,dx=\\frac{27}{3}=9.$$\nIf m≤f(x)≤M on [a,b] with f integrable and a<b, then m(b−a)≤∫f≤M(b−a). Bounds provide a reasonableness check before exact evaluation.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nVertical separation, net accumulation and average level are different quantities. Ordering, interval width and units should tell you which operation fits the problem.\n## Common mistakes\nDetermine which curve is above before subtracting. Solve every relevant intersection and split if the ordering changes. Do not confuse average value with the average of two endpoint values unless a separate argument makes them equal.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich integrand gives the enclosed area?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nupper minus lower, 4x−x², on [0,4].\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nCompute the average of x² on [0,3].\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$(1/3)\\int_0^3x^2\\,dx=3$.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA learner averages f(1)=3 and f(4)=18 to get 10.5.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nan endpoint average is not the integral average here; integrating gives 9.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nIf 2≤g≤7 on [3,8], bound its integral.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\n$10\\le\\int_3^8g(x)\\,dx\\le35$.\n:::end\n\n## Application and PCK\nCompare a variable demand curve with a constant baseline to interpret average value. A PCK teaching suggestion is to ask students to justify units and ordering before accepting a correct number.\n\nUse the common-mistake analysis as a misconception probe and the assessment checkpoint as a formative check.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan an average value lie outside the continuous function’s minimum and maximum on the interval?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nno; the integral bounds place the average between them.\n:::end"
